Superior Vena Cava Obstruction
Palliative & End of Life
Facial/neck swelling, Distended chest collateral veins, Dyspnea worse lying supine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-050
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe facial swelling, suffocating dyspnea when lying flat, and a terrifying sensation of cranial fullness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has developed rapidly progressive swelling of the face and neck over the last 48 hours, culminating tonight in severe, impending airway compromise (stridor) and an inability to lie supine without suffocating. This is a life-threatening oncological emergency. Urgent, immediate after-hours assessment was mandatory to diagnose the obstruction, administer high-dose corticosteroids to reduce the edema, and arrange immediate emergency hospital transfer prior to a complete, fatal central airway occlusion.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head feels like it's going to explode. I can't breathe at all when I lie back, and my voice has gone completely hoarse."
History of Present Illness: Over the past week, the patient has experienced progressive, severe dyspnea distinctly worsening specifically when explicitly bending forward or strictly lying supine. The spouse notes visible, prominent swelling strictly affecting the face, neck, and upper torso (plethora), which has worsened significantly today. The patient complains of a severe, intractable dry cough explicitly secondary directly to profound mechanical tracheal compression. There is a distinctly muffled, hoarse voice explicitly caused by localized recurrent laryngeal nerve compression or profound laryngeal edema. The patient reports a persistent, throbbing headache and a distinct sensation of severe cranial fullness specifically exacerbating completely when stooping. Tonight, the spouse noticed stridor presenting explicitly as a loud, high-pitched inspiratory wheeze indicating critical, impending central airway compromise. The patient has known advanced lung malignancy.
Functional Status: Strictly sitting upright in a chair; completely unable to mobilize due to severe dyspnea and cranial pressure.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impending, fatal airway obstruction; severe panic and distress for both patient and spouse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is sitting bolt upright, leaning forward, visibly anxious, plethoric (red, swollen face), and using accessory muscles to breathe.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals gross conjunctival edema and distinct periorbital swelling. There are engorged, distinctly dilated, and visibly non-pulsatile superficial collateral veins tracking directly across the upper chest and shoulders. Cardiovascular exam reveals distended, highly elevated jugular venous pressure explicitly persisting strictly despite being in a fully upright seated position. Respiratory auscultation reveals a high-pitched inspiratory stridor audible from the end of the bed, indicating critical laryngeal/tracheal narrowing. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed facial/neck swelling, the visibly distended chest collateral veins, and the severe dyspnea strictly worse when lying supine.
Environmental Observations: N/A - Emergency transfer required immediately.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral inhalers and standard analgesia are completely ineffective for this severe mechanical vascular compression.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superior Vena Cava Obstruction - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Oncological Emergency / Impending Airway Collapse)
Differentials:

Acute allergic reaction (Anaphylaxis) - Ruled out/unlikely as the cause of the facial swelling and stridor, given the insidious 48-hour progression, the presence of chest collateral veins, and the lack of urticaria or known allergen exposure.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to the localized nature of the swelling (face/neck only, sparing the lower limbs), and the presence of collateral chest veins which are hallmark for SVCO.

Severe Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of inspiratory stridor (upper airway) rather than purely expiratory wheeze, and the massive facial plethora.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This is a time-critical, catastrophic oncological emergency. The patient is suffering from severe Superior Vena Cava Obstruction (SVCO) leading directly to critical laryngeal edema and impending central airway collapse, evidenced by the objective findings of stridor, severe facial plethora, and hypoxia. Because the tumor is actively strangling the main venous return from the head and the central airway, delaying assessment or managing in the community would result in imminent, fatal asphyxiation or massive cerebral edema. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to administer massive doses of corticosteroids to rapidly shrink the peritumoral and laryngeal edema, and to orchestrate an immediate blue-light ambulance transfer for emergency stenting or radiation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat intravenous Dexamethasone (16mg) immediately to rapidly reduce the central airway edema and decrease the mechanical pressure. Administered stat subcutaneous morphine to blunt the severe panic and reduce the work of breathing. Arranged an immediate Priority 1 ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary hospital. Instructed the patient to remain strictly bolt upright.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the pressure building in your head (trigger), strictly refuse to bend forward or lie back, maintaining a rigidly upright posture (behavior). This strictly utilizes gravity to keep the blood from pooling further in your head and neck, preventing a complete blockage of your airway (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Immediate hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Emergency Medicine, Respiratory, and Radiation Oncology review immediately upon ED arrival.

